 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Steam power plant",
   "text": "Steam at about 550 °C (\\(823\\) K) drives the turbine and the waste heat goes to a river or cooling tower near \\(30\\) °C (\\(303\\) K). Carnot limit: \\(1-303/823={{ec|1}}\\%\\). Real plants reach roughly \\(38\\%\\) (approximate typical value, Beyond the book).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Petrol car engine",
   "text": "Burning petrol gives gas at roughly \\(1173\\) K in the cylinder (an effective figure, not the flame temperature); the cold side is air near \\(303\\) K. Carnot limit: \\({{ec|1}}\\%\\). A real car turns only about \\(28\\%\\) of the fuel energy into motion (approximate, Beyond the book).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 1
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Gas turbine",
   "text": "A jet-style turbine runs at about \\(1473\\) K with air at \\(298\\) K. Carnot limit: \\({{ec|1}}\\%\\) — the hotter, the better. Real simple-cycle turbines manage about \\(35\\%\\) (approximate, Beyond the book).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 2
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Ocean thermal (OTEC)",
   "text": "Warm surface water (\\(298\\) K) and cold deep water (\\(278\\) K) differ by just \\(20\\) K. Carnot limit: \\({{ec|1}}\\%\\), and a real plant gets about \\(3\\%\\). Poor, but the fuel is free (approximate, Beyond the book).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 3
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Your own engine",
   "text": "Choose \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) with the sliders. The Carnot limit is \\(1-T_2/T_1={{ec|1}}\\%\\). Raise \\(T_1\\) or lower \\(T_2\\) and the limit climbs, but it never reaches \\(100\\%\\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "title": "Why is real < Carnot?",
   "text": "The Carnot engine is **reversible**: no friction, no heat leaks, and every stroke so slow that the gas is always in equilibrium. Such an engine has zero power. Real engines must run fast, lose heat through walls and rub against themselves, so they fall short of \\(1-T_2/T_1\\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "eng": 3
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 7000
  }
 ],
