   "explain": "No engine can beat the Carnot efficiency \\(63.2\\%\\) for these temperatures (Carnot theorem).",
   "set": {
    "eng": 0
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "Which change gives a **bigger** Carnot efficiency?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "Raise \\(T_1\\) by 50 K"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "Lower \\(T_2\\) by 50 K"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "b",
   "hint": "See the derivation of \\(\\eta=1-T_2/T_1\\).",
   "explain": "Lowering \\(T_2\\) by \\(\\Delta\\) always gains more than raising \\(T_1\\) by \\(\\Delta\\), because \\(T_2<T_1\\)."
  }
 ],
 "takeaways": [
  "The Carnot efficiency is a **ceiling**, not a promise.",
  "Real engines reach roughly half to two-thirds of it.",
  "A bigger temperature gap means a higher ceiling."
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "Beyond the book",
   "text": "The \"typical real\" efficiencies and the effective temperatures are round textbook-style figures for illustration, not measurements of one particular machine."
  }
 ]
